# Writer Agent Test

*August 12, 2026 · P0 item 3, tested before building*

**The question:** can a writer agent turn a vendor's rambling conversation into something worth reading?

**Method:** three samples of realistic 素材 — the way people actually talk, not the way they'd write. Draft each at the target length. Assess honestly against the bar.

**The bar:**
> Is there a person, and is something at stake for them?
> Would a stranger want to know how it turned out?

Plus: specificity carries the feeling, and **never explain the meaning at the end.**

---

# Sample A — Good material

*The kind of conversation the interviewer should produce on a good day.*

### 素材 (raw)

> **Agent:** How did the weekend go?
>
> **Vendor (Mei, Fortune Noodle):** Sold out by 11. That never happens, usually we go till 2. I had to turn people away which I hate doing.
>
> **Agent:** What sold out?
>
> **Vendor:** The chili oil. I only made 40 jars because last month I made 60 and had 20 left. So this month I thought okay, 40. Wrong. There was a line before I even finished setting up.
>
> **Agent:** Any idea what changed?
>
> **Vendor:** Someone posted about it I think. A girl came up and showed me a video on her phone, it was me? Someone filmed me making it at the demo in July. I didn't know. It has like 90,000 views. I don't have TikTok.
>
> **Agent:** What was it like seeing that?
>
> **Vendor:** Honestly a little strange. My hands look old in it. But my daughter was so excited, she made me watch it four times. She's twelve. She said mom you're famous and I said I'm not famous, I'm out of chili oil.
>
> **Agent:** Are you making more this week?
>
> **Vendor:** I'm making 80. I was up until 1am Tuesday toasting the sichuan peppercorns. My whole apartment smells. My neighbor knocked to check if something was burning.

**Signals:** person ✓ · stake ✓ · scene ✓ · detail ✓ · quotes ✓

### Draft — 187 words

**Headline:** She's not famous. She's out of chili oil.

Mei sold out by 11 on Saturday. She usually goes till two.

She'd made forty jars. Last month she made sixty and carried twenty home, so forty seemed like the sensible number. There was a line before she finished setting up.

A woman in the queue held up her phone to show her why: a video of Mei at the July demo, toasting peppercorns, filmed by someone she never noticed. Ninety thousand views. She doesn't have TikTok.

"Honestly a little strange," she said. "My hands look old in it."

Her twelve-year-old made her watch it four times.

> "She said mom you're famous, and I said I'm not famous, I'm out of chili oil."

This week there will be eighty jars. She was up until one in the morning on Tuesday toasting peppercorns, and at some point her neighbor knocked on the door to ask whether something was burning.

**Assessment: ✅ Clears the bar.**
Person, stake, a real scene, and it ends on the neighbor knocking — concrete, no lesson attached. The daughter's line does the emotional work without anyone naming it.

